"""English — details, curated_1 (61 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_001": "In Surah Hud, after his people had mocked him for years, Nuh is told that no more of them will believe (11:36), and then receives the order: \"And build the ship under Our eyes and Our inspiration, and do not address Me concerning those who have wronged; indeed, they are to be drowned\" (11:37). As he built it, the chiefs of his people passed by and ridiculed him, and he answered that they would be ridiculed in turn (11:38). A Muslim learns from Nuh to keep working at what Allah has commanded even while others laugh.",
"prophets_002": "The scene belongs to Surah Al-Anbiya. Ibrahim had broken his people's idols and, when questioned, pointed to the largest one so that they would see that idols cannot even speak (21:58-65). Their answer was, \"Burn him and support your gods\" (21:68). Then Allah spoke: \"O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim\" (21:69), and the next verse says, \"They intended for him harm, but We made them the greatest losers\" (21:70). A Muslim learns that the fire obeys its Creator, and that whoever stands for Allah's oneness is in His care.",
"prophets_003": "Surah As-Saffat tells that Yunus fled to the laden ship, drew lots and was among the losers (37:140-141); \"then the fish swallowed him while he was blameworthy\" (37:142). The surah adds: \"Had he not been of those who glorify Allah, he would have remained in its belly until the Day they are resurrected\" (37:143-144). Allah cast him onto the bare shore, ill, and made a gourd plant grow over him (37:145-146), then sent him back to his people, who believed (37:147-148). A Muslim learns that turning to Allah in the darkest place is what brings the way out.",
"prophets_004": "Surah Yusuf records the king's words: \"I have seen seven fat cows being eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain and others dry\" (12:43). His courtiers called it a jumble of dreams, but the cupbearer remembered Yusuf, who was still in prison, and went to ask him (12:44-46). Yusuf explained: seven years of sowing, keeping the harvest in its ears, then seven hard years that would eat what was stored, then a year of rain and pressing (12:47-49). A Muslim learns that knowledge given by Allah is to be used to help people, even those who have wronged us.",
"prophets_005": "Surah An-Nisa, listing the messengers Allah sent, singles out one: \"And messengers We have told you about before, and messengers We have not told you about; and Allah spoke to Musa directly\" (4:164). Surah Ta-Ha shows the scene: at the fire in the sacred valley of Tuwa, Musa heard, \"Indeed, I am your Lord, so remove your sandals\" (20:12), and, \"Indeed, I am Allah; there is no god but Me, so worship Me and establish prayer for My remembrance\" (20:14). A Muslim learns that Allah honoured Musa with His speech, and that its first message was worship and prayer.",
"prophets_006": "The verse opens the story of Sulaiman in Surah An-Naml: \"And Sulaiman inherited Dawud. He said, 'O people, we have been taught the speech of birds, and we have been given from all things. Indeed, this is clear bounty'\" (27:16). The surah then shows him hearing an ant warn its colony and smiling, thanking Allah for His favour (27:18-19), and questioning the hoopoe that brought him news of Saba (27:20-22). A Muslim learns that Sulaiman called his gift a bounty from Allah, and met it with thanks rather than pride.",
"prophets_007": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Isra: \"And your Lord is most knowing of whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And We have made some of the prophets exceed others, and to Dawud We gave the Zabur\" (17:55). Surah An-Nisa says the same, \"and to Dawud We gave the Zabur\" (4:163), and Surah Al-Anbiya quotes from it: \"We have written in the Zabur, after the reminder, that the earth will be inherited by My righteous servants\" (21:105). A Muslim believes in the Zabur as a Book of Allah given to a prophet who was also a king.",
"prophets_008": "In Surah Al 'Imran the angels tell Maryam of a word from Allah, a son named the Messiah, 'Isa son of Maryam (3:45). She asks how she can have a child when no man has touched her, and the answer comes: \"Such is Allah; He creates what He wills. When He decrees a matter, He only says to it, 'Be,' and it is\" (3:47). Later the surah gives the comparison: \"The example of 'Isa to Allah is like that of Adam; He created him from dust, then said to him, 'Be,' and he was\" (3:59). A Muslim learns that 'Isa's birth was a sign of Allah's power, not a sign of divinity.",
"prophets_009": "The surah takes its name from the verse that opens her story: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16). The angel came to her in the form of a man and said, \"I am only the messenger of your Lord to give you a pure boy\" (19:19); when she asked how, he answered, \"Thus; your Lord says, 'It is easy for Me'\" (19:21). Surah Al 'Imran says that Allah chose her and purified her above the women of the worlds (3:42). A Muslim honours Maryam as a woman the Qur'an itself names and praises.",
"prophets_010": "Surah Al-Anbiya gives his prayer and its answer: \"And Ayyub, when he called to his Lord, 'Indeed, adversity has touched me, and You are the most merciful of the merciful.' So We responded to him and removed what afflicted him, and We gave him his family and the like of them with them, as mercy from Us and a reminder for the worshippers\" (21:83-84). Surah Sad adds Allah's verdict: \"Indeed, We found him patient, an excellent servant; he was one who kept turning back\" (38:44). A Muslim learns that patience does not mean silence before Allah; Ayyub complained to Him alone, and was healed.",
"prophets_011": "The verse follows Allah's announcement to the angels that He would place a successor on the earth, and the lesson of the names that Adam knew and they did not (2:30-33). Then: \"And when We said to the angels, 'Prostrate before Adam,' so they prostrated, except Iblis. He refused and was arrogant and became of the disbelievers\" (2:34). Surah Al-A'raf records his excuse: \"I am better than him; You created me from fire and created him from clay\" (7:12). A Muslim learns that Adam was honoured by Allah's command, and that arrogance was the first sin.",
"prophets_012": "The title comes at the end of a verse in Surah An-Nisa: \"And who is better in religion than one who submits himself to Allah, does good, and follows the way of Ibrahim, inclining toward truth? And Allah took Ibrahim as a close friend\" (4:125). Surah An-Nahl describes the friend: \"Ibrahim was a nation in himself, devoutly obedient to Allah, inclining toward truth, and he was not of the polytheists\" (16:120). Allah also made him a leader for the people (2:124). A Muslim learns that closeness to Allah is reached by submission and doing good, the two things the verse names.",
"prophets_013": "Surah Yusuf tells that his brothers, jealous that their father loved Yusuf more, first spoke of killing him, until one proposed the well so that a caravan would pick him up (12:8-10). \"So when they took him and agreed to put him into the bottom of the well, We inspired to him: you will surely tell them of this deed of theirs while they do not perceive\" (12:15). They came to their father at night weeping, with false blood on his shirt, and Ya'qub answered, \"Patience is most fitting\" (12:16-18). A Muslim learns that Allah was with Yusuf in the well, and that the story ends with him telling them, exactly as promised.",
"prophets_014": "Surah Hud follows the flood from the building of the ark (11:37) to its end. When the command came and the oven gushed, Nuh loaded two of every kind and those who believed, \"and none had believed with him except a few\" (11:40). The ark sailed \"through waves like mountains\", and Nuh called his son to board, but the son chose a mountain and was drowned (11:42-43). Then: \"O earth, swallow your water, and O sky, withhold\", and the ark settled on al-Judiyy (11:44). A Muslim learns that safety came from obeying Allah, not from family ties or high ground.",
"prophets_015": "Surah Ash-Shu'ara tells that Pharaoh's army pursued the Children of Israel until the two groups saw each other, and Musa's companions cried, \"We are surely overtaken\" (26:61). He answered, \"No! Indeed, with me is my Lord; He will guide me\" (26:62). \"Then We inspired to Musa: strike the sea with your staff, and it parted, and each part was like a great towering mountain\" (26:63). Allah saved Musa and those with him and drowned the others (26:65-66). A Muslim learns from Musa's answer that certainty in Allah stands even when the sea is ahead and the enemy behind.",
"prophets_016": "The case is told in Surah Al-Anbiya: \"And Dawud and Sulaiman, when they judged concerning the field, when the sheep of a people had strayed into it at night, and We were witness to their judgement. And We gave understanding of it to Sulaiman, and to each We gave judgement and knowledge\" (21:78-79). The same verses add that the mountains and the birds were made to glorify Allah with Dawud (21:79), and the next that Dawud was taught to make coats of mail (21:80). A Muslim learns that both prophets were honoured, and that sound judgement is a gift from Allah, not merely a matter of age.",
"prophets_017": "Surah Ta-Ha recalls it to Musa himself: \"When We inspired to your mother what We inspired: cast him into the chest and cast it into the river, and the river will throw it onto the bank; there an enemy of Mine and an enemy of his will take him\" (20:38-39). Then Allah says, \"I bestowed upon you love from Me, so that you would be brought up under My eye\" (20:39); his sister followed and offered a nurse, and he was returned to his mother so that her eye would be comforted (20:40). A Muslim learns that Allah's plan ran through Pharaoh's own house, and that a mother's obedience was rewarded.",
"prophets_018": "Surah Al-A'raf records Salih's words to Thamud: \"O my people, worship Allah; you have no god other than Him. There has come to you clear evidence from your Lord: this is the she-camel of Allah, a sign for you. So leave her to eat in Allah's land and do not touch her with harm, lest a painful punishment seize you\" (7:73). They hamstrung the she-camel and defied him to bring what he threatened (7:77), and the earthquake seized them in their homes (7:78). A Muslim learns that a sign from Allah is to be respected, and that mocking it brings its own end.",
"sira_001": "The Qur'an names the city where the Prophet ﷺ was born: it speaks of \"the valley of Makkah\" (48:24) and calls it \"the Mother of Cities\" (6:92). In Surah Al-Balad Allah swears by it, \"I swear by this city\" (90:1), and the next verse addresses the Prophet ﷺ as one living in it (90:2); Surah At-Tin calls it \"this secure city\" (95:3). Makkah is the town of the Ka'ba, the House that Ibrahim and Isma'il raised (2:127), and the Prophet ﷺ was born among Quraysh, the tribe that kept it. A Muslim learns that the last prophet was born beside the first House built for the worship of Allah.",
"sira_002": "Surah Al-Fil asks the Prophet ﷺ: \"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant? Did He not make their plan go astray? He sent against them birds in flocks, striking them with stones of baked clay, and He made them like straw eaten up\" (105:1-5). The army had come against the Ka'ba, and the surah that follows draws the lesson for its keepers: \"Let them worship the Lord of this House, who has fed them against hunger and made them safe from fear\" (106:3-4). A Muslim learns that the year the Prophet ﷺ was born was already marked by Allah's protection of His House.",
"sira_003": "Khadijah was a merchant of Makkah whose goods the Prophet ﷺ had carried in trade before she proposed marriage to him, and she was the mother of his daughter Fatimah. When he came down from the cave of Hira trembling after the first revelation, it was to her that he said, \"Cover me\", and she who answered that Allah would never disgrace him, because he kept the ties of kinship, bore people's burdens, gave to the poor, honoured the guest and helped those struck by hardship. She died in Makkah before the Hijrah. A Muslim learns from Khadijah that the first believer was a wife who knew the character of the man she believed in.",
"sira_004": "Before his mission, the Prophet ﷺ used to withdraw to the cave of Hira for nights at a time to worship, returning to Khadijah for provisions. There the angel came to him and said, \"Read\", and he answered that he was not one who could read; the angel pressed him hard three times, then recited: \"Read in the name of your Lord who created, created man from a clinging clot. Read, and your Lord is the most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know\" (96:1-5). He returned to Khadijah with his heart pounding, and she wrapped him and reassured him. A Muslim learns that the first word of the revelation was a command to read.",
"sira_005": "Surah Al-Baqarah answers those who took Jibril as an enemy: \"Say: whoever is an enemy to Jibril, it is he who brought it down upon your heart by permission of Allah, confirming what came before it, and as guidance and good tidings for the believers\" (2:97). The next verse names him beside Mikal among Allah's angels (2:98). Surah Ash-Shu'ara calls him \"the Trustworthy Spirit\", who brought the Qur'an down upon the Prophet's ﷺ heart in a clear Arabic tongue (26:193-195). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an came by a trusted messenger from Allah, word for word, to the heart of the Prophet ﷺ.",
"sira_006": "The word hijrah means leaving one's home for another, and the Qur'an gives the emigrants their name from it: \"the first forerunners among the Muhajirun and the Ansar\" (9:100), those who left and those who took them in. Surah Al-Hashr describes them as \"the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His approval\" (59:8), and praises the people of Madinah who loved whoever emigrated to them (59:9). The Prophet ﷺ himself was driven out, and Allah aided him in the cave (9:40). A Muslim dates the years from the Hijrah because that departure founded the community.",
"sira_007": "The Qur'an uses both names of the city. In the account of the Battle of the Trench it quotes the hypocrites: \"O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so return\" (33:13), and elsewhere it speaks of \"the people of al-Madinah and the bedouins around them\" (9:120). Al-Madinah simply means \"the city\": it became the Prophet's ﷺ city. There he built his mosque, and its people, the Ansar, are praised for sheltering and aiding those who emigrated (8:72). A Muslim learns that Madinah is the city that welcomed the Prophet ﷺ when Makkah drove him out, and the city where he was buried.",
"sira_008": "The verse belongs to Surah At-Tawbah: \"If you do not aid him, Allah has already aided him when those who disbelieved drove him out, the second of two, when they were in the cave, and he said to his companion, 'Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us.' Then Allah sent down His tranquillity upon him and supported him with soldiers you did not see\" (9:40). The Qur'an does not name the companion, but every account of the Hijrah is the same: Abu Bakr left Makkah with the Prophet ﷺ and hid with him in the cave while the pursuers searched. A Muslim learns that the calm of the cave came from trust in Allah, and honours the friend who shared it.",
"sira_009": "Surah Al 'Imran reminds the believers: \"Allah had already given you victory at Badr while you were few; so fear Allah, that you may be grateful\" (3:123). The verses that follow recall the Prophet ﷺ telling them that Allah would reinforce them with three thousand angels sent down, and with five thousand if they held firm (3:124-125), and then set the lesson: \"Allah made it only as good tidings for you, that your hearts would be reassured; and victory is not except from Allah\" (3:126). A Muslim learns that Badr is remembered not for numbers, but for the help that came from Allah to a few who stood firm.",
"sira_010": "Surah Al-Fath had promised it: \"You will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, in safety, with heads shaved and hair shortened, not fearing\" (48:27). When the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah at the head of his companions, he rode with his head bowed low in humility, went to the Ka'ba and cast down the idols around it reciting, \"Truth has come and falsehood has departed; indeed, falsehood is bound to depart\" (17:81), and pardoned the people who had driven him out. Surah An-Nasr describes the people then entering the religion of Allah in crowds (110:1-2). A Muslim learns that the greatest victory was met with humility and forgiveness.",
"sira_011": "The Farewell Sermon was delivered at 'Arafat during the only hajj the Prophet ﷺ performed, before the largest gathering of his life. He told the people that their blood and property were sacred to one another as that day, that month and that city were sacred; he charged them to treat women well and to hold to the Book of Allah, by which they would never go astray; and he asked them whether he had conveyed the message. On that day at 'Arafat was revealed: \"This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you\" (5:3). A Muslim learns that the last public teaching of the Prophet ﷺ was about the rights people owe each other.",
"sira_012": "The verse belongs to the passage of Surah Al-Baqarah on fasting: \"The month of Ramadan, in which the Qur'an was sent down as guidance for the people and clear proofs of guidance and criterion. So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it\" (2:185). Surah Al-Qadr names the night within it: \"Indeed, We sent it down in the Night of Decree\" (97:1), and Surah Ad-Dukhan calls it \"a blessed night\" (44:3). The same verse of Al-Baqarah ends by telling the believers to magnify Allah for having guided them (2:185). A Muslim learns that the fast of Ramadan is tied to the Qur'an: the month of the Book is honoured by the fast.",
"sira_013": "Before revelation the Prophet ﷺ travelled with trade caravans, first in the company of his uncle Abu Talib, then carrying the goods of Khadijah to Syria, and his honesty in dealing earned him the name al-Amin, the trustworthy, among the people of Makkah. The Qur'an records the objection of his opponents, \"What is this messenger who eats food and walks in the markets?\" (25:7), and answers that every messenger before him also ate food and walked in the markets (25:20). Surah Al-Mutaffifin condemns those who give short measure (83:1-3). A Muslim learns that honest trade was the daily work of the Prophet ﷺ, and that a believer's truthfulness is tested in the marketplace.",
"sira_014": "The Prophet's ﷺ father, 'Abdullah, died before he was born, and his mother, Aminah, died while he was still a small child, so his grandfather 'Abd al-Muttalib, chief of Banu Hashim and keeper of the well of Zamzam, took him into his care and kept the boy close beside him at his own seat by the Ka'ba. When the grandfather died, the care passed to his son Abu Talib. Surah Ad-Duha recalls this time to the Prophet ﷺ: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). A Muslim learns that Allah watched over the orphan of Makkah through the hands of his family, and honours the care of grandparents.",
"sira_015": "Abu Talib was the brother of the Prophet's ﷺ father, 'Abdullah, and the father of 'Ali. He took his nephew into his household after 'Abd al-Muttalib died, brought him along on a trade journey to Syria while he was a boy, and after the revelation began he stood between him and Quraysh, refusing every demand to hand him over, through the years of the boycott of Banu Hashim. He died in Makkah before the Hijrah, in the same year as Khadijah. The Qur'an says of those years, \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). A Muslim learns that Allah made a loving uncle the shield of His Messenger.",
"sira_016": "Surah Al-Isra opens with the journey: \"Exalted is He who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs. Indeed, He is the Hearing, the Seeing\" (17:1). The word isra means a journey by night, and the surah is named after it. Surah An-Najm tells of what followed, when the Prophet ﷺ saw the angel again \"at the Lote Tree of the utmost boundary\" (53:13-14) and \"saw of the greatest signs of his Lord\" (53:18). A Muslim learns that Makkah and Jerusalem are joined in one night's journey, and that the Prophet ﷺ was shown signs no one else was shown.",
"sira_017": "Fatimah was a daughter of the Prophet ﷺ and Khadijah, and 'Ali, son of Abu Talib, was his cousin, raised in his own household from childhood. Their marriage took place in Madinah, and from it came al-Hasan and al-Husayn, the two grandsons the Prophet ﷺ loved to carry. He said of her, in Sahih al-Bukhari, \"Fatimah is a part of me; whoever angers her angers me.\" She outlived him by only a few months. A Muslim honours Fatimah as the daughter closest to the Prophet ﷺ, and her marriage as the household in which his grandsons grew up.",
"sira_018": "Surah At-Tawbah sets the year: \"Indeed, the number of months with Allah is twelve months in the register of Allah from the day He created the heavens and the earth; of them, four are sacred\" (9:36). Muharram, whose name means \"the sacred one\", is one of those four and the first month of the year; its tenth day, 'Ashura, is a day of fasting. The Muslims counted their years from the Hijrah, so that the year begins with Muharram and the era begins with the migration to Madinah. The Qur'an says the new moons are \"measurements of time for the people and for the hajj\" (2:189). A Muslim learns to see the year itself as a reminder of the Hijrah.",
"quran_001": "The surah is seven verses. It begins, \"In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful. All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:1-2), and ends with the request, \"Guide us to the straight path, the path of those You have favoured, not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray\" (1:6-7). The Qur'an refers to it in Surah Al-Hijr: \"We have given you seven of the oft-repeated and the great Qur'an\" (15:87). The Prophet ﷺ called it the Mother of the Qur'an. A Muslim learns Al-Fatiha first of all, because every prayer is built on it: praise, then a plea for guidance.",
"quran_002": "The Qur'an opens with Al-Fatiha, seven verses, and closes with An-Nas, \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind\" (114:1), and between them stand 112 more, 114 in all. They are not arranged by the order of revelation: the first verses revealed, in Al-'Alaq, stand in the 96th surah, while the long surahs of Madinah stand near the front. Every surah begins with \"In the name of Allah\" except At-Tawbah, the ninth, and the whole is divided into thirty parts so that it can be read through in a month. A Muslim learns the count as the frame of the Book he will spend a lifetime reading.",
"quran_003": "Al-Baqarah, \"The Cow\", takes its name from the story of the cow that the Children of Israel were told to sacrifice and questioned again and again (2:67-71). It holds the longest verse of the Qur'an, on writing down a debt (2:282), and the Throne Verse, \"Allah, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining\" (2:255). It ends with a prayer the believers still recite: \"Our Lord, do not burden us with what we have no strength to bear; pardon us, forgive us and have mercy on us\" (2:286). A Muslim learns that the longest surah is a full course in the religion: belief, law and prayer together.",
"quran_004": "Three verses make up the surah: \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar. So pray to your Lord and sacrifice. Indeed, your enemy is the one cut off\" (108:1-3). It is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ, and al-Kawthar means great abundance: the gift comes first, then the response it asks for, prayer and sacrifice to Allah alone, then the reply to his enemy. Surah Al-An'am gives the same pairing: \"my prayer, my sacrifice, my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (6:162). A Muslim learns that the shortest surah holds the whole pattern of gratitude: Allah gives, and the servant answers with worship.",
"quran_005": "Surah Yusuf opens with the verses of the clear Book (12:1) and then states it: \"Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur'an so that you may understand\" (12:2). The Qur'an repeats the point elsewhere: it came \"in a clear Arabic tongue\" (26:195), and had it been in a foreign language its hearers would have asked why its verses were not made plain (41:44). Every messenger was sent in the language of his people so that he could make things clear to them (14:4). A Muslim learns that the words of the Qur'an are Arabic, and reads a translation as its meaning, not as the Qur'an itself.",
"quran_006": "Surah Maryam opens with five letters, \"Kaf, Ha, Ya, 'Ayn, Sad\" (19:1), then tells of Zakariyya, who called on his Lord in secret for a son and was given Yahya (19:2-11), before turning to Maryam herself: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16). Her son, held in her arms as an infant, speaks his first words in the surah: \"Indeed, I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet\" (19:30). A Muslim learns that the surah named after a mother is a surah about Allah's mercy to those who call on Him.",
"quran_007": "The Qur'an uses the word of its own chapters. It challenges its doubters, \"then produce a surah like it\" (2:23), and introduces Surah An-Nur with, \"A surah which We have sent down and made obligatory\" (24:1). Each surah bears a name taken from a word or a story inside it, Al-Baqarah from the cow, Yusuf from the prophet, An-Nas from mankind; the longest has 286 verses and the shortest three. All but one begin with \"In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful.\" A Muslim learns to find his way in the Book by the names of its surahs.",
"quran_008": "The word ayah means a sign, and the Qur'an uses it for both its verses and Allah's marks in creation. Surah Yusuf begins, \"These are the verses of the clear Book\" (12:1), while Surah Al-Baqarah says that in the creation of the heavens and the earth, the alternation of night and day and the ships on the sea \"are signs for a people who use reason\" (2:164); the Arabic word is the same in both. Surah Fussilat joins the two: \"We will show them Our signs in the horizons and within themselves\" (41:53). A Muslim learns that reading a verse and looking at the sky are the same act: reading the signs of Allah.",
"quran_009": "The hadith is narrated by 'Ubadah ibn as-Samit in Sahih al-Bukhari 756: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"There is no prayer for the one who does not recite the Opening of the Book.\" The Opening of the Book, Fatihat al-Kitab, is Al-Fatiha, the seven verses that the Qur'an calls \"seven of the oft-repeated\" (15:87). It is recited standing, in every rak'ah, before any other surah, and its last verses are the prayer for guidance to the straight path (1:6-7). A Muslim learns Al-Fatiha by heart before anything else, because no rak'ah stands without it.",
"quran_010": "The Qur'an names the one it was sent to: \"Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but the Messenger of Allah and the seal of the prophets\" (33:40), and speaks of \"what was sent down to Muhammad, and it is the truth from their Lord\" (47:2). It came down upon his heart by the Trustworthy Spirit, in a clear Arabic tongue, so that he would be one of the warners (26:192-195), and it came in parts: \"a Qur'an We have divided, that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period, and We have sent it down in stages\" (17:106). A Muslim learns that the Book and the Messenger are received together: the Qur'an is read as it was given to him.",
"faith_001": "The hadith is narrated by Ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 8: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Islam is built on five: the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakat, the hajj, and fasting Ramadan.\" The picture is of a building resting on five supports, with the testimony of faith named first because the other four stand on it. The Qur'an itself pairs prayer with zakat (2:43), commands the fast (2:183) and the hajj (3:97). A Muslim learns the five by name as the frame of the whole religion.",
"faith_002": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8, narrated by Ibn 'Umar, names it first of the five: \"the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" Both halves are in the Qur'an: \"So know that there is no god but Allah\" (47:19) and \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah\" (48:29). Surah Al 'Imran says that Allah Himself, the angels and those of knowledge bear witness that there is no god but Him (3:18). The same two sentences ring out in every adhan and are repeated in every prayer. A Muslim learns that the whole religion opens with a witness spoken by the tongue and held in the heart.",
"faith_003": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8 lists \"establishing the prayer\" as the second of the five on which Islam is built. The Qur'an says that \"prayer has been decreed upon the believers at fixed times\" (4:103), and marks those times: \"Establish prayer from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night, and the recitation of dawn\" (17:78), and \"at the two ends of the day and in the early hours of the night\" (11:114). The five are Fajr, Dhuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib and 'Isha, and every mosque calls to each of them. A Muslim learns that the day is shaped by five appointments with Allah, from before sunrise to after dark.",
"faith_004": "Sahih al-Bukhari 8 places \"giving the zakat\" third among the five on which Islam is built, straight after the prayer. The Qur'an joins the two again and again: \"Establish prayer and give zakat\" (2:43). It tells the Prophet ﷺ, \"Take from their wealth a charity by which you purify them and cause them increase\" (9:103), and the word zakat itself carries both meanings, purity and growth. Surah At-Tawbah names those who receive it, beginning with the poor and the needy (9:60). A Muslim learns that a share of his wealth belongs to others by right, and that giving it cleans what he keeps.",
"faith_005": "The command is in Surah Al-Baqarah: \"O you who believe, fasting has been decreed upon you as it was decreed upon those before you, so that you may become mindful of Allah\" (2:183). The next verse says the days are numbered, and that whoever is ill or on a journey makes up an equal number of other days (2:184); then the month is named: \"The month of Ramadan, in which the Qur'an was sent down\" (2:185). A Muslim learns that the fast is old, shared with the believers before us, and that its purpose is a heart mindful of Allah.",
"faith_006": "The duty is stated in Surah Al 'Imran, just after the Ka'ba is called the first House established for mankind (3:96): \"In it are clear signs, the standing place of Ibrahim; whoever enters it is safe. And due to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House, for whoever is able to find a way there\" (3:97). Surah Al-Hajj tells how the call began, when Allah ordered Ibrahim to proclaim the hajj so that people would come on foot and on every lean camel from every distant pass (22:27). A Muslim learns that the hajj is owed to Allah, once, by whoever can reach His House, and that it answers a call first given to Ibrahim.",
"faith_007": "Surah Al-Baqarah records the change of direction: \"We have certainly seen the turning of your face toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qiblah with which you will be pleased. So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque; and wherever you are, turn your faces toward it\" (2:144). The verses before it foresee the foolish asking what turned the believers from their former qiblah, and answer that to Allah belong the east and the west (2:142). A Muslim learns that facing the Ka'ba is obedience to a verse, and that every mosque on earth turns its rows toward one House.",
"faith_008": "Sahih Muslim 8 is the hadith of Jibril, narrated by 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. A man in very white clothes and very black hair, with no sign of travel on him and unknown to anyone present, sat down before the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about Islam, then about iman. The answer was: \"To believe in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, the Last Day, and to believe in the decree, its good and its bad.\" He asked about ihsan too, and about the Hour; when he had gone, the Prophet ﷺ said, \"That was Jibril; he came to teach you your religion.\" A Muslim learns the six as the content of faith, and the scene as the way faith is taught: by asking.",
"virtues_001": "The hadith is narrated by Abu Hurayrah in Sahih al-Bukhari 6018. It ties three duties to belief in Allah and the Last Day: the believer does not harm his neighbour, honours his guest, and \"speaks good or keeps silent.\" Faith in the Last Day is named because every word will be answered for; the Qur'an says, \"Not a word does he utter except that with him is an observer prepared\" (50:18), and tells the Prophet ﷺ to say to His servants that they should speak what is best (17:53). A Muslim learns to weigh a sentence before speaking it, and to count silence as a good deed when nothing good is there to say.",
"virtues_002": "Sahih al-Bukhari 6018, narrated by Abu Hurayrah, binds the neighbour's rights to faith itself: whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day does no harm to his neighbour. The Qur'an sets the neighbour in the same list as parents and kin: \"Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and to parents do good, and to relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbour, the neighbour farther away, and the companion at your side\" (4:36). Two kinds of neighbour are named, so that the one next door and the one further along are both included. A Muslim learns that treating the neighbour well is part of believing, not a separate courtesy.",
"virtues_003": "In Sahih al-Bukhari 6018 the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, placing hospitality between the rights of the neighbour and the guarding of the tongue. The Qur'an shows the model in Ibrahim: when \"the honoured guests\" came to him, he slipped away to his family, brought a fat calf, set it before them and said, \"Will you not eat?\" (51:24-27). He served them himself, quickly and without being asked. A Muslim learns that a guest at the door is an occasion for faith to show itself, and that welcoming him is a habit of the prophets.",
"virtues_004": "The hadith is narrated by Anas ibn Malik in Sahih al-Bukhari 13: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"None of you believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself.\" The Qur'an calls the believers brothers (49:10) and shows the love it means in the Ansar of Madinah, who \"prefer them over themselves, even though they are in need\" (59:9). The measure is simple: whatever good a person wants for himself, he wants for others too. A Muslim learns that faith is tested by what he wishes for other people, and lets that wish guide his hands.",
"virtues_005": "The hadith is narrated by 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr in Sahih al-Bukhari 10: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"The Muslim is the one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the emigrant is the one who leaves what Allah has forbidden.\" The tongue is named first, because words wound before hands do. Surah Al-Hujurat spells out what the tongue must not do: mock, insult, call people by hurtful names, suspect, spy or backbite (49:11-12). A Muslim learns that the name Muslim, from the root of peace and safety, is earned when others are safe from him.",
"virtues_006": "Surah Al-Isra sets kindness to parents right after the worship of Allah: \"Your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him, and to parents, good treatment. Whether one or both of them reach old age with you, say not to them 'uff', nor rebuke them, but speak to them a noble word\" (17:23). The next verse adds a posture and a prayer: \"Lower to them the wing of humility out of mercy, and say: My Lord, have mercy upon them as they brought me up when I was small\" (17:24). A Muslim learns that even a sigh of impatience toward an ageing parent is too much, and that mercy for them is asked from Allah every day.",
"prophets_019": "Surah Al-Baqarah tells how, after announcing to the angels that He would place a successor on the earth (2:30), \"He taught Adam the names, all of them; then He showed them to the angels and said, 'Inform Me of the names of these, if you are truthful'\" (2:31). The angels answered, \"Exalted are You; we have no knowledge except what You have taught us\" (2:32), and Adam told them the names (2:33). Allah then reminded them that He knows the unseen of the heavens and the earth (2:33). A Muslim learns that knowledge is a gift from Allah, that it raised Adam before the angels, and that the honest answer to a question is the one the angels gave.",
}
